Z||000015||fierofrek||06-26-2001||01:15 AM||||Not true. I didn't want my Fiero when i bought it, but my brother wanted me to get it to match his. after many hours of talk, I went down and got it. Now I wouldn't give it up for anything! best part is, I've only had to replace a fuel pump and thats it!(knock on wood) Icee, If you like the car, and this guy is willing help you out, go for it. Yes older cars need work from time to time (more then newer ones), but so do long term relationships, we gonna start a two week dating trend? LOL. TerryK is right in the aspect that we know what we should and shouldn't do, so do whats best for you.<P>[EDIT] spelling not my strong suit!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by fierofrek (edited 06-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||GT-maniac||06-26-2001||01:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BC-GT:<BR><B>Hell, i would sell my left nut for a carbed<BR>big V8 in my Fiero, what's wrong with carbs?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hmm... how about the pain in the arse it is to ever get them working correctly ? Finding someone that still knows how to adjust carbs.. Poor fuel economy, hard starting in winter etc etc. I've owned lots of carbuerated cars and fuel injected ones too. And while there's nothing quite like the roar of the secondaries opening up at full throttle on a quadrajet, I'll take the increased reliabiltiy and better fuel economy of a fuel injected engine any day.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||terryk||06-26-2001||01:33 AM||||No, it's true. Don't ever question me.<P>What I means is that when you know it's right, you don't need convincing. When there is doubt, you shouldn't. The Fiero is the perfect example.<P>Further interpretion should be left to the theologists.||||reg||12
